WEBVTT

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:05.682
This structured table is easy to scan because every row follows the same columns.

00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:10.022
But the columns do not all have the same analytical meaning.

00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:11.692
Patient ID and ZIP code are labels.

00:00:11.692 --> 00:00:14.317
Date of birth is temporal.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:17.227
Sex and blood type are categorical.

00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:21.122
This is a useful example of the distinction between dataset organization and variable meaning.

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:25.282
The dataset is structured, but each variable still needs its own classification.

00:00:25.282 --> 00:00:29.837
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:37.337
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.222
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:44.937
In a real setting such as a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity,

00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:50.852
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.852 --> 00:00:53.212
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:59.120
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
